# Abstract measures on profinite spaces are determined by clopen masses

Theorem statement: `ProfiniteMeasure.ext_of_clopen_masses` (`fa40f0e8-4ef0-4ab4-9e28-8880cd7ee2ff`),
by Wenqian, 2026-09-06.

Proof: submission `674a3b80-557e-410d-ad5e-aaecc46c2c54`, by Wenqian, 2026-09-06 (ACCEPTED); locally
adapted.

Let X be a compact Hausdorff totally disconnected space and let R be a normed field. Two continuous
R-linear functionals on C(X,R) are equal if they agree on the characteristic function of every
clopen subset of X. This gives the uniqueness mechanism for extending bounded p-adic residue-disk
masses to abstract measures, as required by the Colmez moment-extension frontier of the
Mazur–Tate–Teitelbaum interpolation mission.

## Explanation of the source proof

Let $\mu$ and $\nu$ be the two continuous linear functionals. Their equalizer

$$E=\{f\in C(X,R):\mu(f)=\nu(f)\}$$

is closed. The hypothesis and linearity imply that every finite linear combination of characteristic
functions of clopen subsets belongs to $E$.

For any $f\in C(X,R)$ and any neighborhood of $f$ in the uniform topology, compactness and total
disconnectedness provide a finite clopen step function in that neighborhood. The proof uses
Mathlib's finite clopen-indicator approximation theorem, then packages the resulting step function
as a finite sum of scalar multiples of continuous characteristic functions. Thus every continuous
function belongs to the closure of $E$, hence to $E$ itself. Extensionality gives $\mu=\nu$.

This supplies the uniqueness principle for the ordinary p-adic measure-extension frontier. Applying
it there still requires showing that clopen subsets of the p-adic unit group are finite unions of
residue disks; construction of the measure and verification of its higher moments remain separate
work.

The proof is complete and contains no sorry, added axioms, or platform theorem imports. It passed
Lean 4.33.1 with Mathlib 0df444a360eaa60ab8c11dca51a86af692955474. The compact-convergence
neighborhood argument follows the pattern of Mathlib's ContinuousMap.denseRange_tensorHom proof.

